1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,310
Your change is too big for one review. So
you split it into three pull requests,

2
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:14,403
each built on the one below. A pull request
is GitHub's proposal to merge one branch

3
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:18,405
into another. The reviewer of the
bottom one asks for a small fix.

4
00:00:18,445 --> 00:00:19,132
You make it.

5
00:00:19,472 --> 00:00:24,222
Now the middle branch is built on a commit
that no longer exists on the bottom

6
00:00:24,262 --> 00:00:29,135
branch. So you rebase the middle branch.
Now the top branch is built on a commit

7
00:00:29,175 --> 00:00:33,495
that no longer exists on the middle
branch. So you rebase that as well.

8
00:00:33,535 --> 00:00:36,873
Three rebases, three forced
pushes, for a one-line fix.

9
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:39,820
And tomorrow there'll be
another review comment.

10
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:43,781
Teams give up on stacked pull
requests for exactly this reason.

11
00:00:43,821 --> 00:00:48,024
One option makes it a single command.
Remember that count: three rebases.

12
00:00:49,864 --> 00:00:53,720
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,895
A commit is one saved snapshot of your
project. A branch is a name that points at

14
00:00:58,935 --> 00:01:04,006
one commit. A rebase copies your branch's
commits onto another commit, and every

15
00:01:04,046 --> 00:01:05,155
copy has a new ID.

16
00:01:05,495 --> 00:01:10,130
The last video gave you the todo list,
which a rebase executes from top to bottom,

17
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:14,805
and the eight instructions. Today: four
options that write or extend that list for

18
00:01:14,845 --> 00:01:19,480
you. None of them is a new mechanism. Each
one automates something you could do by

19
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:20,563
hand in the editor.

20
00:01:20,903 --> 00:01:26,536
Four options, four jobs. --autosquash reads
specially named commits and arranges the

21
00:01:26,576 --> 00:01:32,141
list. --autostash deals with uncommitted
changes that would otherwise stop a rebase

22
00:01:32,181 --> 00:01:37,746
from starting. --update-refs moves every
branch in a stack, not only the one you're

23
00:01:37,786 --> 00:01:43,351
on. And --rebase-merges rebuilds a branch
that contains merge commits, commits with

24
00:01:43,391 --> 00:01:47,201
more than one parent, which a
plain rebase would flatten.

25
00:01:47,541 --> 00:01:51,703
For each one you'll see what it adds,
and what it still leaves for you to do.

26
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:56,124
That second half is where the surprises
are, and it's today's interview question.

27
00:01:57,964 --> 00:01:59,542
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:59,882 --> 00:02:01,294
After this video you can:

29
00:02:01,634 --> 00:02:06,215
Record a correction as a fixup commit
and fold it in with --autosquash.

30
00:02:06,555 --> 00:02:11,722
Explain where local changes are while
a rebase with --autostash is stopped.

31
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:17,204
Rebase a stack of branches in one operation
with --update-refs, and publish it.

32
00:02:17,544 --> 00:02:22,988
Keep merge commits through a rebase with
--rebase-merges, and say what replaced

33
00:02:23,028 --> 00:02:24,237
--preserve-merges.

34
00:02:26,077 --> 00:02:31,194
Fixup commits and --autosquash. In one
sentence: a fixup commit is a correction

35
00:02:31,234 --> 00:02:36,677
recorded now, as a commit of its own, whose
subject line names the earlier commit it

36
00:02:36,717 --> 00:02:42,029
belongs to. --autosquash reads those subjects
and arranges the to-do list for you.

37
00:02:42,369 --> 00:02:46,019
git commit 🟢 SAFE creates them,
with three kinds of subject.

38
00:02:46,359 --> 00:02:49,652
Making the correction is
safe: it only adds a commit.

39
00:02:49,692 --> 00:02:53,363
The rewriting happens later,
once, when you run the rebase.

40
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:59,126
--autostash. In one sentence: a rebase
refuses to start while tracked files have

41
00:02:59,166 --> 00:03:04,658
uncommitted changes. --autostash puts those
changes into a stash commit, rebases,

42
00:03:04,698 --> 00:03:09,916
and applies the stash to the result. A
stash is uncommitted work, recorded as

43
00:03:09,956 --> 00:03:12,033
commits that no branch reaches.

44
00:03:12,373 --> 00:03:17,109
The detail that matters: an autostash is
a stash commit that is never put on the

45
00:03:17,149 --> 00:03:21,945
stash list. While the rebase is stopped,
your change is in no file of the working

46
00:03:21,985 --> 00:03:25,289
tree, the folder of files you
edit, and not on the list.

47
00:03:25,329 --> 00:03:29,647
Its ID is in the state directory, the
rebase's own folder inside dot git.

48
00:03:29,987 --> 00:03:35,077
--update-refs. In one sentence: it makes
a rebase move every local branch that

49
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:39,614
points at one of the replayed commits,
not only the branch you're on.

50
00:03:39,954 --> 00:03:44,732
Precisely: for each such branch, Git adds
a line update-ref refs/heads/<name> to the

51
00:03:44,772 --> 00:03:49,435
list, directly after the commit the branch
points at, and sets the branch when the

52
00:03:49,475 --> 00:03:54,138
list is finished. Not moved: branches
checked out in another worktree, tags, which

53
00:03:54,178 --> 00:03:58,956
are names that aren't expected to move, and
remote-tracking branches, your record of

54
00:03:58,996 --> 00:04:00,218
the server's branches.

55
00:04:00,558 --> 00:04:06,758
--rebase-merges. In one sentence: by default
a rebase leaves merge commits out and

56
00:04:06,798 --> 00:04:12,466
lays all other commits out in one line.
--rebase-merges rebuilds the branch

57
00:04:12,506 --> 00:04:18,554
structure, merge commits included. On
screen: today's last demo branch, with its

58
00:04:18,594 --> 00:04:20,229
merge commit, 7537190.

59
00:04:20,569 --> 00:04:25,162
When not to use these? --autostash
when the uncommitted change matters.

60
00:04:25,202 --> 00:04:30,643
The textbook's advice is to commit it as
work in progress first, because a commit is

61
00:04:30,683 --> 00:04:35,732
in the reflog, Git's local list of where
a branch has been, and an edit in the

62
00:04:35,772 --> 00:04:41,083
working tree is not. And --rebase-merges:
first ask whether a branch that contains

63
00:04:41,123 --> 00:04:46,368
merges should be rebased at all. A long-lived
integration branch that others pull

64
00:04:46,408 --> 00:04:47,413
from should not.

65
00:04:49,253 --> 00:04:52,365
Keep the film editor's decision
list from the last video.

66
00:04:52,405 --> 00:04:56,513
Today's four options are assistants who
prepare the list before you see it.

67
00:04:56,854 --> 00:05:02,289
The first assistant reads sticky notes on
the takes, "this one fixes that one", and

68
00:05:02,329 --> 00:05:07,764
sorts the list accordingly. That's --autosquash.
The second clears your desk before

69
00:05:07,804 --> 00:05:11,789
the session and puts everything
back afterwards: --autostash.

70
00:05:11,829 --> 00:05:17,330
The third knows that three reels share
footage, and relabels all three cans when the

71
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:22,806
footage is re-cut: --update-refs. The fourth
writes cutting instructions for a film

72
00:05:22,846 --> 00:05:25,906
with two storylines that
join: --rebase-merges.

73
00:05:26,246 --> 00:05:30,967
Where does the picture break? In the same
place each time: an assistant does what

74
00:05:31,007 --> 00:05:35,846
the notes say, not what you meant. A sticky
note on the wrong take folds a fix into

75
00:05:35,886 --> 00:05:40,196
the wrong commit. A can labelled "backup"
that holds the same footage gets

76
00:05:40,236 --> 00:05:45,016
relabelled with the others. So the habit
from the last video carries over: when it

77
00:05:45,056 --> 00:05:47,896
matters, add -i and read
the list before it runs.

78
00:05:49,736 --> 00:05:54,672
First, the stack before: three branches,
each built on the one below, and main one

79
00:05:54,712 --> 00:05:59,586
commit ahead. A plain rebase of the top
branch would move only the top label, and

80
00:05:59,626 --> 00:06:02,560
leave private copies of
the lower work inside it.

81
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:08,198
After one rebase with --update-refs,
every commit is a new copy, marked with a

82
00:06:08,238 --> 00:06:13,674
prime, and all three labels sit on the
copies. The originals turn dashed: only a

83
00:06:13,714 --> 00:06:15,316
reflog still names them.

84
00:06:17,156 --> 00:06:18,972
Part 1: fixup commits.

85
00:06:19,312 --> 00:06:23,212
Review comment 1: exact_match must
ignore surrounding whitespace.

86
00:06:23,252 --> 00:06:25,212
That belongs in the first commit.

87
00:06:25,552 --> 00:06:30,704
git commit --fixup=HEAD~2 wrote
a commit whose subject is fixup!

88
00:06:30,744 --> 00:06:37,355
followed by the subject of its target.
HEAD~2 is two commits below HEAD, where you

89
00:06:37,395 --> 00:06:42,872
are: the first commit. Comment 2
gets a --squash commit with a note.

90
00:06:42,912 --> 00:06:48,307
And comment 3, which is only about
a message, gets --fixup=reword:.

91
00:06:56,447 --> 00:07:01,657
git rebase -i --autosquash main 🟡
opens a todo list of six lines.

92
00:07:01,997 --> 00:07:07,127
Six commits: three originals and, on top,
three corrections in the order they were

93
00:07:07,167 --> 00:07:11,162
made. Try it now, on paper. Thirty
seconds: write the six lines.

94
00:07:11,202 --> 00:07:11,793
I'll wait.

95
00:07:16,633 --> 00:07:21,849
The list arrives already arranged: every
correction sits directly below its target,

96
00:07:21,889 --> 00:07:25,459
with the right instruction.
fixup under the first commit.

97
00:07:25,499 --> 00:07:30,398
fixup -C under the second, with # empty
marking the commit that carries only a

98
00:07:30,438 --> 00:07:34,895
message. squash under the third. The
editor opens once, for the squash.

99
00:07:38,235 --> 00:07:40,712
Three commits again, with
the corrected messages.

100
00:07:41,052 --> 00:07:46,016
And the last command, a diff from ORIG_HEAD,
the old tip, to HEAD, prints nothing:

101
00:07:46,056 --> 00:07:50,715
the tree at the tip is identical before
and after. Autosquash changes how the

102
00:07:50,755 --> 00:07:54,254
history is cut into commits,
not what the branch contains.

103
00:07:54,594 --> 00:07:57,017
Without -i the list isn't shown at all.

104
00:07:57,357 --> 00:08:02,300
One trap in the configuration.
labs/run ch09/autosquash-config.

105
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:07,205
rebase.autoSquash exists so that
you need not type the option.

106
00:08:07,245 --> 00:08:11,556
Predict what a plain git
rebase main does with a fixup!

107
00:08:11,596 --> 00:08:14,087
commit on the branch. I'll wait.

108
00:08:18,927 --> 00:08:22,435
"Up to date", and the fixup!
commit is still there.

109
00:08:22,475 --> 00:08:25,983
Almost everyone expects a
fold here the first time.

110
00:08:26,023 --> 00:08:31,478
Root cause, from the textbook: the setting
applies to interactive rebases only.

111
00:08:31,518 --> 00:08:37,182
The manual says "by default for interactive
mode". A plain rebase of a branch that

112
00:08:37,222 --> 00:08:42,329
is already on top of main has nothing
to do. Type --autosquash, or use -i.

113
00:08:42,670 --> 00:08:47,209
Part 2: autostash.
labs/run ch09/autostash.

114
00:08:47,549 --> 00:08:50,083
"Cannot rebase: You
have unstaged changes."

115
00:08:50,423 --> 00:08:53,500
"Created autostash", three
picks, "Applied autostash".

116
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:57,309
The edit is back in the working
tree, and the stash list is empty.

117
00:08:57,649 --> 00:09:03,504
So where is your change while a rebase is
stopped at a conflict, waiting for you to

118
00:09:03,544 --> 00:09:07,552
decide a file's content?
labs/run ch09/autostash-stopped.

119
00:09:07,592 --> 00:09:12,666
Quick quiz, three options: in your
files, on the stash list, or neither?

120
00:09:12,706 --> 00:09:13,802
Say it out loud.

121
00:09:18,642 --> 00:09:23,745
Neither. It's in no file of the working
tree, and not on the stash list.

122
00:09:23,785 --> 00:09:29,531
Its ID is in .git/rebase-merge/autostash,
and git stash show -p on that ID prints

123
00:09:29,571 --> 00:09:33,031
your edit. The two ways
out treat it differently.

124
00:09:33,371 --> 00:09:39,469
--abort 🟡, which discards resolution
work in progress as you saw in video 53,

125
00:09:39,509 --> 00:09:42,657
applies the autostash: the edit is back.

126
00:09:42,997 --> 00:09:47,732
--quit creates a stash entry and says so:
"Your changes are safe in the stash."

127
00:09:47,772 --> 00:09:51,843
Now it's on the list, and the conflict
is still in the working tree.

128
00:09:52,183 --> 00:09:57,614
The last step can itself conflict, when the
rebase brought in a change to the lines

129
00:09:57,654 --> 00:10:00,184
you had edited. Back to ch09/autostash.

130
00:10:00,524 --> 00:10:05,268
Read the last line of the rebase output
first: the rebase succeeded and the branch

131
00:10:05,308 --> 00:10:08,943
has moved. What conflicted is the
stash application afterwards.

132
00:10:09,282 --> 00:10:14,848
Git kept the stash on the list, and the
markers are labelled Updated upstream, the

133
00:10:14,888 --> 00:10:20,316
rebased branch, and Stashed changes, your
edit. Resolve the file, then git stash

134
00:10:20,356 --> 00:10:25,169
drop 🔴, as in the stash video: only
once the content is safely on disk.

135
00:10:25,509 --> 00:10:32,215
Part 3: a stack and --update-refs.
labs/run ch09/update-refs.

136
00:10:37,755 --> 00:10:43,038
Three branch labels on one line of five
commits: the stack from the opening, with

137
00:10:43,078 --> 00:10:48,032
real IDs. First a plain rebase of the
top branch. Predict which labels move.

138
00:10:48,072 --> 00:10:48,689
I'll wait.

139
00:11:01,329 --> 00:11:05,846
Only feat/ingest-chunker moved. The two
lower branches still point at the old

140
00:11:05,886 --> 00:11:09,988
commits, and the top branch now
contains private copies of their work.

141
00:11:10,028 --> 00:11:14,663
That's the state from the opening. Now,
after going back to the start, with the

142
00:11:14,703 --> 00:11:15,077
option.

143
00:11:15,417 --> 00:11:20,604
Two update-ref lines in the list, each
directly after the commit its branch points

144
00:11:20,644 --> 00:11:23,919
at. A ref is Git's word for
a name such as a branch.

145
00:11:23,959 --> 00:11:29,019
And at the end: "Updated the following
refs with --update-refs", with both lower

146
00:11:29,059 --> 00:11:29,975
branches named.

147
00:11:33,315 --> 00:11:38,303
One line, and all three labels on the new
commits. The reflog of a moved branch says

148
00:11:38,343 --> 00:11:42,913
"rewritten during rebase", and its @{1},
the value one change ago, is the old

149
00:11:42,953 --> 00:11:43,332
commit.

150
00:11:43,672 --> 00:11:48,427
Now the assistant doing what the labels say.
You make a "backup" branch and a tag at

151
00:11:48,467 --> 00:11:52,423
the tip before a risky rebase. Predict
what each points at afterwards.

152
00:11:57,263 --> 00:12:03,961
backup/chunker is in the list of updated
refs. It now points at the new tip,

153
00:12:04,001 --> 00:12:09,900
2925218. It no longer backs anything
up. The tag stayed on 4b52940.

154
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:15,660
Root cause: a "backup" made with git branch
is a branch that points into the rebased

155
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:19,235
range, so --update-refs moved
it along with the others.

156
00:12:19,275 --> 00:12:24,696
Before a risky rebase, mark the old tip with
a tag, or write down the ID, or rely on

157
00:12:24,736 --> 00:12:25,411
the reflog.

158
00:12:25,751 --> 00:12:31,731
The undo is also per branch. git reset --hard
ORIG_HEAD 🔴 restores the branch you're

159
00:12:31,771 --> 00:12:36,174
on. Each of the others has to be
put back from its own reflog.

160
00:12:36,514 --> 00:12:42,274
Publishing is per branch as well, in one
command. labs/run ch09/update-refs-push.

161
00:12:42,314 --> 00:12:46,999
A forced push 🔴, one that replaces
commits on the server's branch.

162
00:12:47,039 --> 00:12:52,871
The five answers were given in the video
on forcing a push, and the two guards are

163
00:12:52,911 --> 00:12:53,085
on.

164
00:12:53,425 --> 00:12:57,153
Three "(forced update)" lines.
Every ref gets its own lease.

165
00:12:57,493 --> 00:13:04,720
Part 4: merges. labs/run ch09/rebase-merges.
feat/ingest contains a merge of a side

166
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:05,332
branch.

167
00:13:05,672 --> 00:13:08,968
Predict the shape of the branch
after a plain git rebase main.

168
00:13:13,808 --> 00:13:16,631
Four commits in a line, and
the merge commit is gone.

169
00:13:16,971 --> 00:13:22,120
Whatever existed only in the merge commit,
such as a conflict resolution or a fix

170
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:27,374
made while merging, is not replayed. So
the conflict it resolved comes back at one

171
00:13:27,414 --> 00:13:30,769
of the single commits. Now
with -r, after going back.

172
00:13:31,109 --> 00:13:35,142
The list has become a small graph
program. Read it line by line.

173
00:13:35,182 --> 00:13:38,896
label onto names the new base.
reset onto moves HEAD there.

174
00:13:38,936 --> 00:13:43,923
After the first pick, label branch-point
remembers where the side branch forks.

175
00:13:43,963 --> 00:13:46,405
The side commit is picked and labelled.

176
00:13:46,745 --> 00:13:52,322
Then reset branch-point goes back, the
mainline commit is picked, and merge -C

177
00:13:52,362 --> 00:13:57,939
7537190 feat-ingest-cleaner makes a new
merge with the message of the old one.

178
00:13:57,979 --> 00:14:03,556
Labels are temporary refs under
refs/rewritten/, deleted when the rebase ends.

179
00:14:03,896 --> 00:14:05,917
The diamond is back, on top of main.

180
00:14:06,257 --> 00:14:11,491
Two details. First, the branch feat/ingest-cleaner
still points at the old commit

181
00:14:11,531 --> 00:14:15,463
dc5df93. -r rebuilds commits,
it doesn't move other branches.

182
00:14:15,503 --> 00:14:19,826
Add --update-refs for that. Second,
the new merge is a fresh merge.

183
00:14:19,866 --> 00:14:25,100
The manual states that conflict resolutions
and manual amendments in the original

184
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:27,704
merge commits have to be redone by hand.

185
00:14:28,044 --> 00:14:34,933
The textbook marks this as outdated advice:
git rebase -p or --preserve-merges, and

186
00:14:34,973 --> 00:14:41,695
pull.rebase=preserve, appear in older
tutorials. The option was deprecated in Git

187
00:14:41,735 --> 00:14:48,039
2.22 and removed in 2.34. Git 2.55
answers with the fatal message on screen.

188
00:14:48,079 --> 00:14:51,629
Use --rebase-merges and
pull.rebase=merges.

189
00:14:53,469 --> 00:14:55,117
Five mistakes to watch for.

190
00:14:55,457 --> 00:15:00,571
Setting rebase.autoSquash and expecting
a plain git rebase main to fold fixups.

191
00:15:00,611 --> 00:15:05,855
Root cause: the setting applies to interactive
mode only, and a branch already on

192
00:15:05,895 --> 00:15:08,073
top of main has nothing to replay.

193
00:15:08,413 --> 00:15:12,702
Looking for autostashed work in git
stash list during a stopped rebase.

194
00:15:12,742 --> 00:15:17,762
Root cause: the autostash is a stash commit
recorded in the state directory, put on

195
00:15:17,802 --> 00:15:21,054
the list only by --quit or
when applying it conflicts.

196
00:15:21,394 --> 00:15:26,095
Making a safety copy with git branch
before a rebase with --update-refs.

197
00:15:26,135 --> 00:15:31,493
Root cause: a branch that points into the
range is moved with the others; a tag is

198
00:15:31,533 --> 00:15:31,757
not.

199
00:15:32,097 --> 00:15:35,994
Undoing a stack rebase with one
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD.

200
00:15:36,034 --> 00:15:41,599
Root cause: ORIG_HEAD restores only the
current branch; every other moved branch has

201
00:15:41,639 --> 00:15:42,600
its own reflog.

202
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:47,180
Expecting --rebase-merges to keep
an old merge's conflict resolution.

203
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:52,329
Root cause: the merge is made afresh, and
resolutions or amendments in the original

204
00:15:52,369 --> 00:15:54,252
merge commit have to be redone.

205
00:15:56,092 --> 00:16:01,302
Now, out of the lab. Two practices from the
textbook, both from teams that review in

206
00:16:01,342 --> 00:16:02,114
small pieces.

207
00:16:02,454 --> 00:16:08,203
During a review, answer each comment
with a fixup commit and push normally.

208
00:16:08,243 --> 00:16:14,146
The reviewer sees every response as a
small diff, and nothing is force-pushed

209
00:16:14,186 --> 00:16:20,552
meanwhile. Before the merge, one git rebase
-i --autosquash and one force push fold

210
00:16:20,592 --> 00:16:26,727
everything. A CI job, an automated check,
that fails while a subject starts with

211
00:16:26,767 --> 00:16:31,744
fixup!, squash! or amend! keeps
unfolded corrections out of main.

212
00:16:31,784 --> 00:16:35,680
The check in Lab 9.3 is one
line of git log --grep.

213
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:41,174
And the stack from the opening? A large
change reviewed as a chain of small pull

214
00:16:41,214 --> 00:16:46,303
requests lives or dies by --update-refs,
because every review fix in the lowest

215
00:16:46,343 --> 00:16:51,432
branch has to ripple upward. git config
set rebase.updateRefs true makes it the

216
00:16:51,472 --> 00:16:56,756
default. Now count again. The three rebases
and three pushes of the opening become

217
00:16:56,796 --> 00:17:01,236
one rebase on the top branch, and
one push that names three branches.

218
00:17:01,576 --> 00:17:07,128
On autostash, a caution. rebase.autoStash=true
makes it the default, and git pull

219
00:17:07,168 --> 00:17:12,582
--rebase --autostash does the same for
pulls. The cost is that uncommitted work

220
00:17:12,622 --> 00:17:15,482
travels through a mechanism you don't see.

221
00:17:17,322 --> 00:17:24,239
Your turn. Do Lab 9.3, "Autosquash
fixups", in lab-manual/m09-rebase.md.

222
00:17:24,579 --> 00:17:26,495
Predict before you run the rebase:

223
00:17:26,835 --> 00:17:29,621
The subject line of each
correction commit you create.

224
00:17:29,961 --> 00:17:34,427
The todo list that --autosquash will
open, line by line, with the verbs.

225
00:17:34,767 --> 00:17:39,105
The output of git diff --stat
between the old tip and the new tip.

226
00:17:39,145 --> 00:17:40,166
Think about why.

227
00:17:40,506 --> 00:17:45,652
The challenge is Lab 9.7, "--update-refs
on stacked branches", in the same file.

228
00:17:45,692 --> 00:17:50,579
Before the rebase there, list every
local branch that points into the range,

229
00:17:50,619 --> 00:17:54,145
including any you didn't think
of as part of the stack.

230
00:17:55,985 --> 00:18:02,432
Q158: "What do --update-refs and
--rebase-merges each add to a plain rebase, and

231
00:18:02,472 --> 00:18:05,838
what does each still leave for you to do?"

232
00:18:08,178 --> 00:18:13,286
Answer out loud first. The question has four
cells: two options, and for each "adds"

233
00:18:13,326 --> 00:18:17,821
and "leaves". A strong answer starts
from what a plain rebase does in each

234
00:18:17,861 --> 00:18:21,683
situation, so that the addition
is a contrast and not a slogan.

235
00:18:21,723 --> 00:18:25,605
For "leaves", think along three
lines. Which refs are not moved.

236
00:18:25,645 --> 00:18:30,692
What is not carried over from the old
history. And what publishing and undoing then

237
00:18:30,732 --> 00:18:35,718
require. An answer that fills all four
cells from the todo list itself, naming the

238
00:18:35,758 --> 00:18:38,476
lines each option writes,
is a senior answer.

239
00:18:40,316 --> 00:18:43,243
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

240
00:18:43,583 --> 00:18:48,149
A fixup commit is an ordinary commit
whose subject names its target, and

241
00:18:48,189 --> 00:18:51,858
--autosquash places it under
that target in the todo list.

242
00:18:51,898 --> 00:18:55,568
The configuration setting covers
interactive rebases only.

243
00:18:55,908 --> 00:19:00,818
--autostash parks uncommitted changes in
a stash commit that is recorded in the

244
00:19:00,858 --> 00:19:05,769
state directory, applied at the end or
on abort, and moved to the stash list by

245
00:19:05,809 --> 00:19:07,272
--quit or by a conflict.

246
00:19:07,612 --> 00:19:12,521
--update-refs moves every local branch
that points into the replayed range,

247
00:19:12,561 --> 00:19:17,601
including a branch meant as a backup,
and undo and publishing are per branch.

248
00:19:17,941 --> 00:19:23,961
A plain rebase flattens merges. --rebase-merges
rebuilds them with label, reset and

249
00:19:24,001 --> 00:19:28,342
merge lines, as fresh merges.
--preserve-merges was removed.

250
00:19:30,182 --> 00:19:34,284
Read sections 9.7 to 9.10 of Chapter 9.

251
00:19:34,624 --> 00:19:41,678
Do Exercise 9.2, Level 1, "fold a fix into
the commit it belongs to", and Exercise

252
00:19:41,718 --> 00:19:48,945
9.6, Level 2, "a merge inside the branch",
both in exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

253
00:19:49,285 --> 00:19:54,309
Today you let four assistants write the todo
list, and you know what each one leaves

254
00:19:54,349 --> 00:19:57,565
for you. Practise the fixup
lab before the next video.

255
00:19:57,605 --> 00:20:01,785
Next time: conflicts during a rebase,
who "ours" is, and the ways out.

256
00:20:01,825 --> 00:20:04,920
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

257
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:06,367
See you in the next one.
